ed radical photoreduction couples acetate and water to produce glycolic acid.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Liu et al. (2021): demonstrated surface UV photoredox conversion.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_015\",\n      \"name\": \"Peracetic acid amination to glycine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_008\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [null],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"370-400K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"high\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": null\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Peracetic acid reacts with ammonia; displacement of peroxide group by ammonia forms glycine.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Aubrey et al. (2008): documented as a productive hydrothermal amino acid synthesis sequence.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"pathways\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"name\": \"Aminonitrile hydrolysis (cyanosulfidic surface route)\",\n      \"description\": \"From surface photoproduced formaldehyde and cyanosulfidic acetone pools, glycine is generated via Strecker aminoacetonitrile, which hydrolyzes to glycine. Glycine then reacts with formaldehyde (aldol or Schiff base/hydrolysis) to yield L-serine.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_008\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P2\",\n      \"name\": \"Surface cyanosulfidic Strecker to 2-amino-3-hydroxypropanenitrile, hydrolysis to serine\",\n      \"description\": \"From glycolic acid (UV or Cannizzaro formaldehyde), conversion to aminonitrile intermediate, then surface hydrolysis to serine.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_013\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_010\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_014\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P3\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrothermal glyoxylate reductive amination\",\n      \"description\": \"Glyoxylate is reductively aminated by hydroxylamine and iron, yielding glycine; glycine then condenses with formaldehyde at hydrothermal vents to form serine.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_011\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_004\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_008\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P4\",\n      \"name\": \"Surface photochemical glyoxylate then reductive amination route\",\n      \"description\": \"2,2-dihydroxyacetic acid is dehydrated by UV to yield glyoxylate, which undergoes iron-catalyzed reductive amination to give glycine, then to serine.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_012\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_004\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_008\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P5\",\n      \"name\": \"Surface N-methylene glycine Schiff base pathway\",\n      \"description\": \"Glycine plus formaldehyde form N-methylene glycine, which upon formaldehyde addition and hydrolytic reduction yields serine.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_005\", \"rxn_006\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_007\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P6\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrothermal peracetic acid route\",\n      \"description\": \"Peracetic acid and ammonia combine to form glycine, which enters the main hydrothermal glycine-formaldehyde condensation to serine.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_015\", \"rxn_004\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_008\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P7\",\n      \"name\": \"Surface acetate photoredox to glycolic acid, Strecker aminonitrile route\",\n      \"description\": \"UV photochemistry converts acetate into glycolic acid, which forms the aminonitrile intermediate, feeding into hydrolytic production of serine.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_014\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_010\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_014\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P8\",\n      \"name\": \"Formaldehyde direct aldol/Cannizzaro loop route\",\n      \"description\": \"CO2 is converted to formaldehyde by surface photoreduction, which then dimerizes via Cannizzaro to glycolic acid, entering Strecker chemistry to serine.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_013\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_010\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_012\", \"mol_014\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P9\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrothermal isocitrate \u2192 glyoxylate \u2192 glycine route\",\n      \"description\": \"Isocitrate undergoes iron-catalyzed cleavage to glyoxylate and succinate; glyoxylate is aminated to glycine, which couples with formaldehyde to give serine.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_011\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_004\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_008\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P10\",\n      \"name\": \"Surface dihydroxyacetic acid \u2192 glyoxylate \u2192 glycine (cyanosulfidic cross-link)\",\n      \"description\": \"Starting from dihydroxyacetic acid (e.g., from sugars), UV-driven dehydration produces glyoxylate, feeding into the hub for both reductive amination and further glycine-mediated pathways.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_012\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_006\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_008\", \"mol_007\"]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"hub_molecules\": [\n    \"mol_005\",\n    \"mol_008\",\n    \"mol_010\",\n    \"mol_012\"\n  ],\n  \"convergence_points\": [\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_008\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_003\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_015\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_004\", \"rxn_005\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_010\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_011\", \"rxn_012\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_008\", \"rxn_007\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_012\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_008\", \"rxn_013\", \"rxn_014\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_009\"]\n    },\n    {\n