rated as forming N-methylene glycine under moderate surface temperatures."}, {"id": "rxn_006", "name": "N-methylene glycine + formaldehyde + water to L-serine", "inputs": ["mol_007", "mol_005", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_017"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": [null], "conditions": {"temperature": "323K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "24h"}, "mechanism": "Retro-[1,3]-prototropic shift and hydrolysis/reduction of imine intermediate with formaldehyde addition yields serine.", "reasoning": "Krishnamurthy et al. (2016): established as a major route to serine on surface environments."}, {"id": "rxn_007", "name": "Glyoxylate reductive amination to glycine", "inputs": ["mol_010", "mol_011"], "outputs": ["mol_008", "mol_004"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["Elemental Iron (Fe)", "Ferrous iron (Fe2+)"], "conditions": {"temperature": "343K", "pressure": "high", "catalyst": "Iron species", "aqueous": true, "other": null}, "mechanism": "Reductive amination of glyoxylate by hydroxylamine and iron, yielding glycine and water.", "reasoning": "Muchowska et al. (2019): iron-catalyzed glyoxylate amination observed experimentally in hydrothermal conditions."}, {"id": "rxn_008", "name": "Glyoxylate reduction to glycolic acid", "inputs": ["mol_010"], "outputs": ["mol_012"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["Ferrous iron (Fe2+)"], "conditions": {"temperature": "343K", "pressure": "high", "catalyst": "Fe2+", "aqueous": true, "other": null}, "mechanism": "Aldehyde reduction of glyoxylate to primary alcohol group, forming glycolic acid.", "reasoning": "Muchowska et al. (2019), Ritson (2021): both support this route both for hydrothermal and cyanosulfidic surface settings."}, {"id": "rxn_009", "name": "Aminonitrile hydrolysis to 2-amino-3-hydroxypropanenitrile", "inputs": ["mol_012", "mol_003", "mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_014"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": [null], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-320K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Cyanosulfidic/evaporitic pool"}, "mechanism": "Glycolic acid (after oxidation to glycolaldehyde in situ) with HCN and NH3 yields the aminonitrile intermediate.", "reasoning": "Patel et al. (2015): 2-amino-3-hydroxypropanenitrile forms by Strecker-type reaction from glycolaldehyde, HCN, and NH3. Glycolic acid can photochemically equilibrate with glycolaldehyde under prebiotic conditions."}, {"id": "rxn_010", "name": "2-amino-3-hydroxypropanenitrile hydrolysis to L-serine", "inputs": ["mol_014", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_017", "mol_018"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": [null], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-320K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": null}, "mechanism": "Nitrile hydrolysis sequence yields serine plus ammonia.", "reasoning": "Patel et al. (2015): major experimentally proven hydrolytic conversion at surface pools."}, {"id": "rxn_011", "name": "Isocitrate cleavage to glyoxylate + succinate", "inputs": ["mol_016"], "outputs": ["mol_010", "mol_019"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["Ferrous iron (Fe2+)"], "conditions": {"temperature": "343K", "pressure": "high", "catalyst": "Fe2+", "aqueous": true, "other": null}, "mechanism": "Fe2+-promoted retro-aldol cleavage splits isocitrate to glyoxylate and succinate.", "reasoning": "Muchowska et al. (2019): prebiotically inspired hydrothermal production of glyoxylate."}, {"id": "rxn_012", "name": "2,2-dihydroxyacetic acid UV photodehydration to glyoxylate", "inputs": ["mol_015"], "outputs": ["mol_010", "mol_004"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Ultraviolet radiation"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-320K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "UV flux 200-300 nm"}, "mechanism": "UV-driven dehydration eliminates water to yield glyoxylate.", "reasoning": "Liu et al. (2021): photochemistry proven on surface pools."}, {"id": "rxn_013", "name": "Formaldehyde Cannizzaro disproportiona