dration.\"\n      ]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"best_pathway_id\": \"P8\",\n  \"overall_confidence\": 0.84,\n  \"network_feedback\": \"The network demonstrates in-depth literature alignment, with all major prebiotic pathways to L-serine included and correctly placed in the appropriate environments (surface and hydrothermal). The environmental consistency is excellent, with nearly all catalysis and transformation types matching experimental precedents. The central issue is poor atom/bond balancing in 10 of 15 reaction nodes\u2014multiple reactions are not mass/charge balanced as written according to the deterministic check. These issues, while generally involving missing water, O2, or H2 as (by-)products, violate chemical feasibility until corrected. No isolated subgraphs are detected, and the network is highly coherent, efficiently interconnecting feedstocks and hubs. Pathways containing fully literature-supported mechanisms (notably P3 and P8) are rated as 'approved'\u2014fixing balance in other pathways would raise the overall score above the approval threshold. Once reaction mass/charge balance is ensured, the network should be considered excellent.\"\n}", "parsed_response": {"pathway_evaluations": [{"pathway_id": "P1", "reaction_scores": {"rxn_009": 0.6, "rxn_010": 0.9}, "chemical_feasibility": 0.7, "pathway_coherence": 0.8, "environmental_progression": 0.95, "target_achievement": 0.95, "overall_confidence": 0.78, "feedback": "P1 (Aminonitrile hydrolysis, cyanosulfidic) matches the Patel et al. (2015) pathway to serine, but reaction formulas are not always balanced. rxn_009 (cyanosulfidic Strecker to 2-amino-3-hydroxypropanenitrile) is unbalanced: O+2 atoms on reactant side, could be corrected by specifying water or O2 as a product. Mechanistically, this is a well-established surface pathway. The hydrolysis step to serine is experimentally demonstrated and appropriately modeled. Fixing the balance issue would raise this pathway to approval.", "approved": false, "problemat